%N A401600 Irregular table read by rows: Place n points at the integer polar angles of the Archimedean spiral r = k, 0 <= k < n, then around the k-th point draw a circle whose radius is the distance from the k-th point to the (k+1)-th point: T(n,k) is the number of k-sided finite regions formed, for n >= 2, k>=2, in the resulting graph.
%C A401600 Other than the n = 2 case, the first graph to contain a 2-sided region is for n = 28. For the graphs studied the number of 2-sided regions is only 0, 1 or 2. It's it not known if there can be more. Likewise the most number of sides for any region in these graphs is 10; it is plausible this is the most number of sides possible for all n. See the attached formatted table text file and image.
%C A401600 See A401497 for further details and images.
%H A401600 Scott R. Shannon, <a href="/A401600/b401600.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 2..1574</a>
%H A401600 Scott R. Shannon, <a href="/A401600/a401600.jpg">Image for n = 28</a>. This is the first case, other than n = 2, where a 2-sided region is present - it is just outside the second circle at about the 2 o'clock position.
%H A401600 Scott R. Shannon, <a href="/A401600/a401600.txt">Formatted table for n = 2..200</a>.
